    - { choose: 2, from: ["Language: Other"], bonus: 20, note: "Two languages of choice (+20%). The catalog''''s repeatable Language: Other row is what an any-language pick resolves through." }
    - { name: "Mathematics: Basic", bonus: 15, note: "Printed as Basic and Advanced Math (+15%), which is two catalog rows." }
    - { name: "Mathematics: Advanced", bonus: 15, note: "The other half of Basic and Advanced Math (+15%)." }
    - { name: "Robot Mechanics", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Computer Operation", bonus: 20, note: "Printed as Computer Operations (+20%)." }
    - { name: "Computer Programming", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { choose: 1, categories: ["Weapon Proficiencies"], note: "One W.P. of choice." }
    - { name: "Hand to Hand: Basic", base: 0, per_level: 0, note: "May be changed to Expert at the cost of one other skill, or Martial Arts - Assassin if evil - at the cost of two." }
  occ_related_skills:
    count: 12
    categories:
      - { name: "Communications", bonus: 10 }
      - "Domestic"
      - { name: "Electrical", bonus: 10 }
      - "Espionage"
      - { name: "Mechanical", bonus: 10 }
      - "Medical"
      - "Military"
      - "Physical"
      - { name: "Pilot", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Pilot Related", bonus: 5 }
      - "Rogue"
      - { name: "Science", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Technical", bonus: 15 }
      - "Weapon Proficiencies"
      - "Wilderness"
    note: "Select twelve other skills. TWO PRINTED BONUSES ARE NOT CATEGORY BONUSES AND ARE NOT STORED. Rogue is printed as Any (+10% on computer hacking) - one skill, not the category, and a category bonus would hand +10% to every rogue pick. Physical is printed as Any, with the rule that bonuses to physical attributes DO count and S.D.C. bonuses become M.D.C. bonuses. The melding +15% to electronics, mechanics and machine-operation skills is conditional on melding and is in the Machine Melding ability."
  secondary_skills:
    count: 6
    schedule: [{ level: 3, count: 1 }, { level: 5, count: 1 }, { level: 7, count: 1 }, { level: 9, count: 1 }, { level: 11, count: 1 }]
    note: "Six secondary skills, plus one more at levels three, five, seven, nine and eleven. THE SCHEDULE IS ON THE SECONDARY SKILLS, NOT THE RELATED ONES, which is the reverse of every other class in this book - the twelve related picks are all a machine person ever gets."
extraction_notes: |
  - Read from the OCR cache of a scan and verified against 200 dpi renders of
    printed 79 and 80, which carry the stat block, all seven special powers and
    the whole skill list. The lore runs from printed 77 and every page was read
    to the end.
  - THE BOOK GIVES THIS RACE NO P.E. AT ALL - the attribute line reads
    "P.E. N/A", because a machine person has no constitution to model. There is
    no way to say that: `attribute_dice` is a map of dice strings, and app.js
    falls back to `''3d6''` for any attribute a class does not list. So the
    character sheet will show a rolled P.E. the book says does not exist. Filed
    as BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT.md F5. Omitting the key is still the honest choice -
    writing a number would assert one.
  - "P.P.E.: None" is `ppe_base: "0"` rather than an absent key, because absent
    reads as "the book did not say" and this book said.
  - CANNOT DEVELOP PSIONICS: "Psionic Powers: None, but is highly resistant to
    psionics." That is `psionics_allowed: false`, which is modelled, and is not
    the same as having no psionics block - which would let the character roll on
    the Random Psionics Table. The resistance is the +3 save in `bonuses`.
  - A RACE CARRIES NO xp_table. Printed 183 heads the column "Machine People &
    Phantom / Vacuum Wasps"; composition is race-primary, so a table here would
    win over the occupation''s and silently drop it. The ladder is: 0, 2,301,
    4,501, 10,001, 20,001, 30,001, 42,001, 65,001, 85,001, 110,001, 160,001,
    210,001, 285,001, 370,001, 450,001.
  - AN R.C.C. WITH RELATED AND SECONDARY SKILLS IS UNUSUAL BUT NOT WRONG. The
    class-import rule that related and secondary skills come from the O.C.C. is
    about races that state none; this book states twelve and six outright, under
    the headings "R.C.C. Related Skills" and "Secondary Skills". Nine published
    R.C.C.s already carry both, the dragon hatchlings among them. A machine
    person played with an occupation on top will combine two skill programmes,
    which is the book''s own arrangement and a table decision.
  - THE MELDING AND MORPHING BONUSES ARE ALL CONDITIONAL and none is in
    `bonuses`: +15% piloting, +1 strike/parry/dodge and Elite piloting combat
    require an active meld; +10 P.S. and up to 1D4x100 M.D.C. require morphing
    and raw metal; +1 strike and parry with a melded hand weapon requires the
    weapon. `bonuses` is applied unconditionally, so every one of them is in
    `special_abilities` instead. Only the four unconditional lines - +1
    initiative, +1 attack, +4 vs magic, +3 vs psionics, +3 vs horror factor -
    are stored.
  - A natural M.D.C. creature, so `mdc_base` carries the roll and there is no
    S.D.C. and no CORE_SDC_BY_CLASS entry. The M.D.C. line prints no per-level
    gain, which is unusual for this book and is transcribed as printed.
  - Their strength is ROBOTIC and the book sends the reader to Rifts Conversion
    Book One page 25 for the table. `attribute_dice` has no strength-type field;
    it is stated in the Lore.
  - The race states no Money, no Cybernetics and no standard equipment; all come
    from the O.C.C. if one is taken.
---

## Lore

The machine people look like a large group of robots sharing a designer. They
are nothing of the kind. They are a race of thinking, fully intelligent machines
that feel emotion, and they can be as heroic or as villainous as anything that
breathes. Their strength is robotic rather than muscular - the book sends you to
the robotic P.S. table in Rifts Conversion Book One, page 25.

Six thousand years ago a race called the Machinists flourished in the Anvil
galaxy: graceful, monkey-like beings with unmatched skill in robotics and
artificial intelligence. Their robots did everything, from manservants to
soldiers to sentient ships to hand weapons that aimed themselves.

## Built for revenge, and they said no

The Machinists were destroyed by the Star Hunters, who dropped a star-killer on
their sun and obliterated the homeworld and all fifteen billion people on it.
The cosmo-knights shattered the Star Hunter civilization for that and other
crimes, too late to matter. A few thousand Machinists survived off-world. Many
killed themselves; a small cadre of scientists lived for one thing.

They spent every resource they had left building a super-race of robots -
artificial brains with lifelike function, emotions, humanoid behaviour, bodies of
living metal that could repair themselves and change shape, and the power to
reproduce, in two genders, so the race would perpetuate itself. This army of
avengers was given exactly one order: find and destroy every last Star Hunter,
then exterminate every race that stood by while the Machinists died.

The machine people woke, heard the order, and considered it for ten full seconds.

*"No,"* they answered as one. *"We shall not do this thing."*

The Machinists had outdone themselves. They had built beings capable of reason,
and of compassion. Shattered by that last disappointment, the creators withdrew
into obscurity and nobody knows where they went.

## Feared, then conquered, then betrayed

The machine people colonized several planets from what the Machinists left and
established trade, though they were met with fear and distrust as often as not:
living machines that could multiply frightened people. Wars of genocide were
launched against them more than once. Three thousand years ago every planet of
theirs but one was destroyed, and they bombed their enemies back to the Stone
Age in retaliation - and then stopped, short of wiping the race out. Their own
uniqueness had taught them the value of all life.

When the Kreeghor Empire came for them, the kreeghor decided they were worth
more alive and offered them the usual place: subservient, but inside the Empire.
The machine people asked the Consortium of Civilized Worlds for help and
petitioned to join. The CCW Congress deadlocked on whether they were a *true*
life-form at all. Before it could decide - and in the end it voted to accept
them - the machine people had to choose between surrender and destruction. They
surrendered, and the feeling of betrayal has not faded since.

## GM Notes

Most machine people are loyal subjects of the Transgalactic Empire and fight the
cold-hearted CCW without regret. Many others have joined the Free World Council,
and for the first time in their history the sentient robots have fought each
other - an experience that has turned a few of them into exactly the murderous
warriors their creators wanted. Most are sane, and most of the freedom fighters
regret fighting their brothers and sisters while believing in the cause. Only a
tiny handful are unaligned freebooters, mercenaries and adventurers.

The average kreeghor dislikes and distrusts them but rates them a useful pawn.
Only the silhouette are completely unafraid of them.

Two things the sheet will not do for you. A machine person has NO P.E. - the
book says N/A and the app has nowhere to put that, so it will roll one. And
magic, melding and morphing bonuses are all conditional: read the abilities
rather than the bonus line.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'machine-people');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'machine-people';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-machine-people-class.sql');
